A||||13||Nick||Engine running hotter than usual...help
Z||000000||Nick||07-15-99||01:17 PM||||I'm new here, so be patient.  <br>Here's the situation:  I have an 86 GT that has been in storage for about four years now, and I just started driving it recently, getting it ready to sell.  It seems to be heating up much faster than it used to.  Because it has been so long since I drove it on a regular basis, I can't be 100% sure that it didn't run this hot before, but I'm pretty sure it didn't.  If I remember correctly, it used to run with the temp guage at the midpoint(the long dash) between 100 and the 220 mark.  Now, it heats up and runs anywhere between there and 220 while I'm going down the road at 50mph+.  If I stop and have to start again, it heats up and then slowly cools back down once I get up to speed.  If I set at a stop light, it will go up steadily(but slowly) until I start moving again.  The fan doesn't kick on until the guage reads about 230-235.  I have AC, but it blows warm, so I can turn it on to get the fan to kick on, but I never had to do that before now.  So far, I have had the coolant system flushed and refilled, replaced the thermostat(195 degree) and it still runs the same.  I don't know what to do next.  Is there a way to get the fan to kick on at a lower temp?  Should I try a 180 degree thermostat?  The guage seems to fluctuate much more than it used to.  It will move back and forth even while I'm going down the road.  Any help or suggestions would be greatly appreciated.
Z||000001||PaUL||07-15-99||03:25 PM||||Your overheating problem is most likely caused by the fan not turning on soon enough. I dont have AC but on my car the fan doesnt switch on until the engine is almost boiling over. I rigged a switch to manually turn the fan on. However you say that it still overheats when going over 50mph so the fan may not be the problem. The only time I need to turn my fan on is when Im idling or moving very slowly.
Z||000002||batboy||07-15-99||05:35 PM||||Your water temperature is running too hot, obviously. Doesn't sound to me like the fan is the problem, sounds like the fan is working the way it's suppose to. I'd try another thermostat. My 87 Fiero has gone through two thermostats in three years. They're cheap and easy to replace. Carefully check for any leaks. Even a pinhole leak in a hose will cause it to lose pressure and run hotter. Sometimes radiators get clogged up, especially if the vehicle sat for a long time. Sorry, I'm sure this is obvious, but is there enough coolant in the system? Is it the proper mix of water and antifreeze? Sometimes those obvious things will cause heating problems. Hope this gives you some ideas to work on. Good luck.
Z||000003||PaUL||07-15-99||07:35 PM||||It could also be that your radiator is all clogged up. You say that you havent driven it in a long time. The inside of the radiator could be corroded. A few years ago, my fiero (also an 86)was overheating, I got a brand new radiator to replace the orig. one and this fixed the problem. 
Z||000004||Carrolles||07-16-99||06:33 AM||||Did you purge all the air out of the cooling system properly?  The procedure for the Fiero is different than most cars since the engine is in the back.
Z||000005||Nick||07-16-99||09:37 AM||||I had the flush and fill done at a local shop, where they told me that they had done Fieros before, and knew how to do it properly.  I'm assuming that if the radiator had been clogged, or if there were any leaks, or anything else wrong with the system, they would have been able to see it and would have told me about it.  Then again, this is just a local shop, and sometimes those types of places can have some pretty ignorant employees.  The thing that bothers me the most is that I don't recall ever having a problem with the engine running hot when I drove the car on a daily basis four years ago.  I seem to remember it running at the same temp. all the time, unless I was sitting in a traffic jam for like a half hour or something.  Now that needle fluctuates so much and it's really annoying.  <p>I am planning on trying a 180 degree thermostat, and maybe installing a lower temp. fan switch, or just having the fan run all the time.  Are there any other options than aren't going to cost me a ton of money?  Also, where can I get a lower temp. fan switch other than the Hypertech. at Summit Racing?  Could I go to the Pontiac dealership and get one?  
Z||000006||batboy||07-16-99||05:43 PM||||I'm afraid that wiring the fan to run constantly is not fixing the real overheating problem. Assuming the gauge is in good working order, fluctuating temperature like you are describing just about has to be either a bad thermostat or low coolant. Thermostat can be faulty right out of the box in rare cases. Also, don't assume that the shop did everything right on the flush and fill. These shops nowadays are so busy that mechanics sometimes get in a hurry and forget to do something that they should of done. Also, they probably would not have inspected the inside of the radiator on a routine flush and fill. Go to a good radiator shop and have them look at it, they're the experts on the cooling system. Pinhole leaks are a bitch to find sometimes. Once time a while back, I was adding coolant to one of my cars every couple of weeks, so I knew I had a leak somewhere. Looked and looked, but couldn't find it, about drove me crazy. Finally, I looked in the right place immediately after I turned the engine off and finally saw the tiny leak. 
Z||000007||Nick||07-23-99||07:15 AM||||UPDATE:<br>Well I just put in a 180 deg. thermostat, and now the car seems to run at the temp. it used to run at while going down the road.  It tends to stay right at 165 most of the time, unlike what it was doing before with the fluctuation.  If I let the car idle for about 5 minutes, it will start to heat up, but it seems to take longer to get real hot.  <p>I still would like to get a lower temp. fan switch installed.  Anyone done this?  I know Hypertech makes one that turns on at 180.  It says in the description that it should be used with their powerchip, and a 180 degree thermostat.  I don't have the chip, can I still use the switch without any problems.  I would think so, but if anyone knows differently, let me know.<p>As far as the suggestions I've gotten, I don't believe that there are any leaks, as I have never seen any evidence of one, and every time I check the level, it is right up full.  I suppose there still could be something clogged up, but I don't think so.<br>Thanks for all the help guys.<br>
Z||000008||theogre||07-23-99||08:24 AM||||the thermostat is what regulates the engine temp. The single speed fan specified turn on is 235F.<p>I run my fan all the time. Even on 90-100 degree days the temp stays in a very narrow range.<p>Don't depend only on the temp gauge when measuring engine temp for this type work. Use an ECM scanner, and read It's sensor. You'll find the engine temp is about 10-20F above the thermostat value. This is normal becuase the ECM temp is measured in a differant part of the engine.<p>The operating temp reported to the ECM with a 195 the range is usually 205-215F, with a 185 stat it should be about 195-205F. ((Those ranges are with the fan running, A/C off, on a coolling system in proper working order.)) These ranges are both within what the ECM thinks are normal temp ranges with a stock chip. A lower temp fan switch and a 185 stat could make the motor run cold, but the only way to be sure is to check things with the ECM scanner. 
Z||000009||Nick||07-23-99||08:55 AM||||Thanks for the info.  What exactly is an ECM scanner?  Is it one of those hand-held computer things that you plug into the ECM?  I think I might know somebody who has one, but if not, can I just take it to a shop and have it checked there?  <br>
Z||000010||Nick||07-29-99||11:03 AM||||I really need to know this info.  So somebody please respond!<p>Does anyone know anything about the low temp fan switch made by Hypertech?  It turns the fan on at 180, but it says that it should be used with Hypertech's powerchip and their 180 deg. Powerstat.  I don't have either one, but I do have a 180 deg. thermostat.  Will it work?  
Z||000011||DJRice||07-29-99||01:06 PM||||Im not exactly sure how to explan this but,here we go. The normal temperature for the computer to turn the fan on is somwhere around 220-235 degrees.  If you change the sensor, without changing the CPU, the engine will not reach proper operating temperature, and the computer will continue to run the engine in a warm up mode.  It could cost you some fuel efficiency but you will probably notice a bit more power.  The hyperchip probably optimizes the entire system for the lower operating temperature.......<p>THe ecm scanner will tell you what temperature the sending unit is telling the ECM.  As I recall, ogre said the ran about $150.<p>hope this helps.<p>
Z||000012||Nick||07-29-99||01:35 PM||||The temp. for the computer to turn the fan on is 235, which in my opinion is way too high.  I don't understand what you mean by not reaching normal operating temperature.  What exactly is normal operating temperature?  If you ask me, an engine that gets to 235 has gone way past normal operating temperature.  I thought that with a 180 degree thermostat, the normal operating temp. should stay right around 180.  Is this wrong?  So are you saying that a 180 degree switch is too low, and maybe I could use one that turns the fan on at around 200?  To me that would make sense.  The thermostat opens up at 180, the engine would normally operate at that temp. and when the engine gets hotter, the fan kicks on to cool it back down to around 180. <p>I don't know, maybe my thinking is way off. Please help me out here. 
Z||000013||theogre||07-29-99||02:30 PM||||Allot of people think the water temp and block temp are the same, but they aren't. the operating temp of the motor actually varies depending on just where you measure it. the thermostat, and gauge/light sensor only sense water temp because of their placement. The ECM sensor is in the head, which commonly runs warmer than the water by 10-20F. Keep in mind the heat in the cylenders has to travel thru the block and head to get to the water. Cast Iron isn't the greatest conductor of heat. <p>The ECM doesn't control the fan. the fan is controlled by it's own isolated circuit. There is yet another sensor unit for the gauge and temp light. The fan temp switch comes on at 235F, and shuts off at 220something(single speed fan).<p>The ECM has no clue about what thermostat, or fan switch is in the motor. It uses it's own sensor to read engine temp and will work correctly as long as that sensor reports the motor in the range it should be. That range will be fine with a 180 thermostat most of the time on a 4 cyl. I'm not sure what the placement of the ECM sensor is on the 6 or what it's offset to the water temp is. If the 6's sensor is placed where it sees water temp more than block temp, it could read low. Without the ECM scanner you won't know what the ECM sensor reading is. The ECM will report Open Loop operation even after warmup if the sensor reads too low.<p>The AutoXray GMonly ECM scanner is around 150$us. It is about 1 pound, and plugs into the ALDL connector by the lighter. It does much more that read trouble codes.
